+## #3132 - A None lead-in stands the wall arc entry down
+
+**What was decided.** On an outside contour, the Arc entry does not run its
+vertical wall arc when the lead-in is None. The level descends by the
+grazing dive at the ring's start, the same dive that a Plunge entry, a
+tabbed level or a blocked arc uses. A note says so once:
+"entry: the arc stands down with no lead-in - ...".
+
+**Why.** With no lead, the wall arc lands on the seam itself. Its start is
+about one arc length back along the seam's tangent (8.7 mm on the user's
+part). From above, in the viewport and in the posted chords, that is a
+straight move into the seam: the same shape as the seeded 8 mm linear lead.
+This is the only setting on `Milling-test.step` that reproduced the report:
+Plunge, Ramp and Helix entries with both leads None already start the cut
+on the ring. The maintainer's law on the issue is that a None lead adds no
+XY lead move at all.
+
+**Considered and dropped.**
+- Keep the arc and only add a note. The user would still see the lead
+  that they refused.
+- Turn the arc into the plane normal to the wall. It still adds XY travel
+  at the seam.
+- Stand the bore-side (Side::Inside) Arc entry down too. In a bore, the
+  ramp, helix and arc are certified descents in bounded air. They are
+  entries, not leads, and the issue keeps entry moves. Not changed.
+- Stand the arc down when a requested lead was refused for room. That is
+  not the user's None, and it is the pre-existing shape. Not changed.
+
+**Cost to change later.** One condition in `contour.rs` (`no_lead_in`).
+A non-centre-cutting tool with Arc entry and no lead now meets the
+graze's centre-cutting refusal. That refusal's hint ("pick the Arc
+entry") does not help when the lead-in is None; a wording follow-up can
+name the lead.
+
+## #3133 - Own blocked PRs first: who fixes, and which PRs are skipped
+
+**What was decided.**
+- AGENTS.md has the new law **Own blocked PRs first**, in the Issue Law
+  before **Claim before any code**.
+- A listed PR whose issue has `claimed` is skipped: it is already in work.
+- An issue worker does not fix a listed PR itself. It stops before the
+  claim and reports the PR numbers; the coordinator sends a worker to each
+  one (work-issue step 0). The coordinator runs the same check before each
+  dispatch for a new issue.
+- "Refresh only at merge time" keeps its rule for maintenance and
+  reviewers; the author's agents refresh only under the new law.
+- The #681 comment format gets a `reload` part: each running session
+  reloads `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/agents/` and `.claude/skills/`, and a fork
+  runs `fork-sync`.
+
+**Why.** Without the `claimed` skip, a second worker sees a PR that a
+first worker is fixing and stops or starts a second fix of it. A worker
+holds one issue and one worktree; a fix of a different PR inside the same
+run breaks that and the effort tier of the brief.
+
+**Considered and dropped.** The worker fixes the listed PRs itself before
+its issue: dropped, because the PR may be above its tier and it doubles
+the worktrees of one run.
+
+**Cost to change later.** A few lines in work-issue and the coordinator
+agent file.
+
+## #3141 - Move the wide-pocket parallel-lane test to the motion heavy lane
+
+**What was decided.** Mark
+`wide_rectangular_pocket_is_cleared_by_parallel_lanes` in
+`crates/motion/tests/adaptive_troch_parallel.rs` with
+`#[ignore = "heavy lane: ..."]`. The release tier and `cargo xtask gate
+heavy` run it with `--ignored` in the step `motion heavy:
+adaptive_troch_parallel`. The test keeps its two pockets (30 x 24 mm D6 and
+44 x 36 mm D10), all its shape assertions and its 90-second budget. The
+budget is now a guard on the release run. Add the light twin
+`wide_rectangular_pocket_is_cleared_by_parallel_lanes_light`, which checks
+the 30 x 24 mm D6 pocket only, in release. This replaces the rule of
+decision #2990 "Apply this assertion in every run" for this test.
+
+**Why.** The debug test ran in 36, 64, 106, 140 and 145 seconds on
+unchanged motion code (#3116, #3141). The post-merge runner was loaded on
+the red runs. Wall time on a loaded runner is not a function of the code,
+so the debug budget made main red with no defect. AGENTS.md "Testing" says
+that a test above about 60 s in debug moves to the heavy lane. In release
+the same work is much faster, so the 90-second budget still catches a real
+slowdown of the planner. The light twin keeps one parallel-lane shape check
+at review and merge time. The `corner_cleanup_reduces_34x16_rest_to_at_most_hsm_rest`
+test stays in the default debug run.
+
+**Considered and dropped.**
+- A larger debug budget. Any number can fail on a loaded runner, and a
+  large number does not catch a slowdown.
+- A smaller fixture. Decision #2990 already measured the smaller D10
+  rectangles: a 38 x 33 mm pocket failed the rest-area assertion. A smaller
+  pocket weakens the case that a D10 tool needs a short side above 32.36 mm.
+- Removing the budget. It is the only guard on the planner time of this case.
+
+**Cost to change later.** The batch gate no longer runs the two wide-pocket
+checks in debug. If the planner becomes fast enough in debug, remove the
