+## #3146 - Rest pass keeps corner reliefs whose start is on the rest center boundary
+
+**What was decided.** `rest_pocket_with_relief` keeps a planned corner relief
+when its start point is inside the rest center area grown by
+`RELIEF_START_TOL` (0.01 mm). Before, it used a bare `Region::contains` on
+the rest center area. Each relief that the rest pass does not keep now gives
+a toolpath note: `corner relief at (x, y) is not cut: the rest area does not
+reach that corner`.
+
+**Why.** The relief start is a vertex of the pocket's own center area. Where
+the rest area touches a corner, that vertex is on the boundary of the rest
+center area. A point-in-region test on a boundary point gives `true` or
+`false` by rounding. On a sharp 30 x 30 mm pocket, it kept 1 of 4 dogbones.
+The tolerance is the same two arc tolerances as `REST_MARGIN`. The start
+stays a legal tool position: it is always on the plain pocket's own center
+area, so the tolerance cannot move the tool past a wall.
+
+**Considered and dropped.**
+- Test the distance from the relief corner to the rest area. This asks the
+  right question in other words, but needs a new distance helper on
+  `Region`. The grown-region test gives the same answer here with the
+  existing API.
+- Keep every relief of the full plan in the rest pass. A sharp inside corner
+  always has rest material after a larger round tool, so this is nearly
+  always right. But a relief forced by an override on a corner with no rest
+  material would then add a spur to a pass that does not go near it. The
+  note is the visible answer for that case.
+- A refusal for a dropped relief. The rest pass is still correct without
+  the relief; the plain pocket is the workaround. A note keeps the program.
+
+**Cost to change later.** Low. The filter and the note are in one block of
+`rest2d.rs`, pinned by `crates/motion/tests/rest_corner_relief.rs`.
+
+## #3150 - The gate pins the env that enters the cargo unit hash
+
+**Cause.** Cargo 1.100 puts each workspace unit in its own directory,
+`target/<profile>/build/<package>/<hash>/` (rlib or test exe, pdb,
+fingerprint). The hash covers the unit's profile. Read in the shared build
+worktree `issue-917` on 2026-11-21:
+
+- The manifests, `Cargo.lock` and `.cargo/config.toml` were the same at
+  the last six heads that were gated there.
+- The `profile` hash in the fingerprint of the same test unit
+  (`world_init_race`) took three values over five gate runs (A, B, A, B,
+  C). Each value gave all of about 600 `camcraft-app` units a new
+  directory. Third-party units did not change: their profile has no
+  incremental setting, and they stayed Fresh.
+- The agents that use that worktree ran gates with different env. One
+  worker restarted its gate with `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0`; the others used
+  the default (incremental on for workspace members). `CARGO_INCREMENTAL`
+  sets `profile.incremental`, which is part of the profile hash.
+- Nothing in `xtask/src` sets a profile, features, `--config` or rustflags
+  for a gate step. Inside one run, the per-crate `-p` builds (#2727) give
+  the lower crates one variant for each feature resolution. That set is the
+  same in each run, so a second run reuses it.
+
+**Decided.** Every cargo call that the gate starts (`direct::cargo`, and the
+catalog builds that already take the gate cache env) gets
+`CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0` and loses the caller's `CARGO_PROFILE_*` and
+`CARGO_BUILD_INCREMENTAL` variables (`xtask/src/unit_env.rs`). The gate
+prints the pin at its start, names each removed variable, and notes a set
+`RUSTFLAGS` / `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` / `CARGO_BUILD_RUSTFLAGS`. The
+summary prints the number of unit directories that the run added (0 on a
+second run of the same tree).
+
+**Why incremental off, not on.** sccache cannot cache an incremental unit,
+so with incremental on the workspace crates never hit the shared cache.
+Incremental data also adds to the size of each unit, and disk is the
+defect here. The coordinator already set `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0` for all
+builds in the shared worktree. Cost: an edit of `camcraft-app` recompiles
+the crate in full instead of in part.
+
+**Dropped.**
+- Remove `RUSTFLAGS` too: a person can set it on purpose (a target CPU, a
+  sanitizer). The gate notes it instead.
+- Prune the old unit directories after each run: an older unit can be the
+  valid one for another head in a shared worktree, and a wrong delete costs
+  a full rebuild. A prune command can follow if the pin is not enough.
+- Fix the env only in the agent briefs: each new agent or shell can miss
+  it again; the gate is the one choke point.
+
+**Cost to change later.** One constant (`unit_env::PINNED`). A change of the
+pinned value makes each existing unit stale once.
+
+## #3169 - The gate removes superseded test-harness units at the end of each run
+
+**Cause.** Read in the shared build worktree `issue-917` on 2026-11-21 (read only):
+
+- One run does not give one target hundreds of hashes. The 634 `camcraft-app`
+  unit directories that were left after a sweep are 627 distinct targets: one
+  unit for each integration test binary (`crates/app/tests` has about 620
+  suites), plus the lib, the bins and the lib tests. Each test binary is its
+  own linked executable with its `.pdb`, about 100 MB, so one set is about
+  64 GB. Only 5 targets had a second variant, from a cargo call outside the
+  gate with another dependency feature resolution.
+- The growth is across trees and env. `camcraft-motion` had 747 units for 124
+  targets (six variants), `camcraft-post` 554 for 110, `camcraft-sim` 462 for
+  92. The variants differ in the profile hash (agents with other env before
+  #3154) or in the fingerprints of registry dependencies (other `serde_core`
+  feature sets). `Cargo.lock` and the manifests were the same at the heads
+  `8b4209215` and `cc04d7bcd`.
+- Cargo never removes a superseded variant. So each change of the env or of
+  a feature resolution adds a full set, about 60 GB for `camcraft-app`. The
+  count "629 to 4386" included these older sets.
+
+**Decided.** At the end of each gate run (green, red or void; never in a dry
+run), `xtask/src/unit_sweep.rs` removes a unit directory only when all of
+these are true:
+
+- it is under `target/debug/build` or `target/release/build` of the gate's
+  own root, and no `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`, `CARGO_BUILD_TARGET_DIR` or
+  `CARGO_BUILD_BUILD_DIR` points elsewhere;
+- its one target fingerprint is a test-harness target (`test-lib-`,
+  `test-integration-test-`, `test-bin-`);
+- another variant of the same target (profile, package, fingerprint name) was
+  written in this run, and this variant was written before the run started;
+- cargo's locks of each profile directory are free (the sweep holds them while
+  it deletes), and no other process names `<root>/target` in its command line
+  (another gate's xtask, a cargo or rustc there, a test executable);
+- no level of the path (`target`, the profile, `build`, the package, the unit,
+  `fingerprint`) is a symlink, a junction or another reparse point. A linked
+  profile directory or `target/` stops the whole sweep; a linked package or
+  unit is skipped. The unit is checked again just before its delete.
+
+The process check matches each form of `<root>/target`: the path as given,
+its absolute and clean form, and the canonical form (a subst drive, a
+junction, `/var` and `/private/var` on macOS). On Windows the match ignores
+case. A root form that cannot be resolved, a process list that cannot be read,
+or a cargo lock that cannot be probed counts as "unsure": nothing is removed.
+
+A failed delete is a warning line, never a gate failure. The summary
+prints the number of directories and the GB removed, or why the sweep did not
+run. `CAMCRAFT_GATE_SWEEP=off` (or `0`) turns it off.
+
+**Why at the end, and in any outcome.** A variant that this run wrote is the
+one that this tree uses. The superseded variant is used again only when the
+worktree goes back to a tree with the earlier resolution; then cargo builds
+those targets again (minutes) instead of keeping 60 GB. A red test does not
+make the new units wrong, so the outcome does not change the choice.
+
+**The lock of `cargo xtask` itself.** `cargo xtask` is `cargo run -p xtask`.
+Cargo holds the build lock of `target/debug` only while it compiles, and frees
+it before it starts the binary. The proof by reading: each gate step starts a
+cargo build into the same `target/debug` while `cargo xtask` is alive; if the
+outer cargo held the lock, every gate would wait for it without end. So the
+lock probe at the end of a run sees only other builds.
+
+**Why only test-harness units.** A test executable is built only when its
+own package is selected, and the gate selects one package for each step, so a
+target has one valid variant in a tree. A library can have two valid variants
+in one tree (for `-p geometry` and for `-p motion`); removing one costs a
+compile in the next run. Libraries are small next to the test executables.
+
+**Dropped.**
+- Fewer `camcraft-app` test binaries (one harness with modules): this is the
+  root of the size, but it changes about 620 files and the gate maps. It can
+  be a separate issue.
+- Remove every unit that this run did not touch: a quick run builds only some
+  crates, and the units of the others are still valid for the tree.
+- Keep the newest variant by time alone: a run that reuses an older variant
+  as Fresh would lose it and build it again in the next run.
+- `debug = "line-tables-only"` for tests: smaller `.pdb`, but it changes the
+  profile, so each unit is stale once, and it changes debugging for persons.
+
+**Cost to change later.** One module; `CAMCRAFT_GATE_SWEEP=off` disables it
+without a code change.
+
+## #3170 - The catalog hint breaks a size tie by name
+
+**Decided:** `tool_gate::catalog_suggestion` keeps its order: the entry of
+the family's first stocked kind nearest 6 mm, then the smaller size. When
+two entries still tie, the entry with the lower name wins. This is a
+deterministic rule, not a preference for one tool line. It makes the hint
+independent of the file order of `assets/tools/catalog.json`.
+
+**Why:** #3159 added "Router ball nose 6 mm" before "BN-0600" in the
+catalog file. The old tie kept the first entry in file order, so the
+refusal for a ball-nose operation changed from 'BN-0600' to the router
+cutter, and `the_refusal_names_a_fitting_catalog_entry` failed on main.
+The taper kind has the same risk: "Router V-bit 60 deg 6 mm" and "Router
+V-bit 90 deg 6 mm" come before "CM-0600-90". The name rule gives
+'BN-0600', 'EM-0600-4FL' and 'CM-0600-90' for any file order. The pin
+`a_size_tie_picks_by_name_not_file_order` covers the two orders of the
+ball-nose pair and the reversed catalog for ball-nose, end mill and taper.
+
+**Dropped:**
+- Change the test expectation to 'Router ball nose 6 mm'. The hint would
+  still depend on file order, and the next catalog edit could move it
+  again.
+- Move the router entries after the metal ladder in `catalog.json`. This
+  hides the fault; the order stays load-bearing.
+- Prefer an entry by its material group. The suggestion has no material
+  context, and this is new behaviour for a P2 fix.
+
+## #3176 - Import empty layout blocks and object dictionaries
+
+**Decision.** Accept an empty block definition for model space or a reserved paper space layout. Check each supplied owner against its named block record. Accept a dictionary entry when its target is another dictionary and its ownership chain ends at a root.
+
+**Reason.** These records can accompany model-space geometry without adding a drawable object. An exporter can omit an owner field or list an empty dictionary only through its parent pointer. The reader can check both forms with a bounded, iterative traversal.
+
+**Dropped.** Skipping either section as a whole would hide block geometry, external references or drawing objects. Checking only familiar dictionary names would miss equivalent metadata from other exporters.
+
+**Cost to change.** Supporting another block or object type requires its own field checks and tests for its effect on imported geometry.
+
+## #3180 - Use one selected DXF chain for a Wire Contour
+
+**Decision.** The operation stores the existing sketch entity and stable chain ID. The resolver keeps the ordered analytic segments, their original primitive indices and the stock-face placement. Generic generation accepts a closed chain of straight segments with coincident joints. The existing wire generator applies the machine condition's signed kerf offset once. The post checks the nominal profile through the full height of the box stock.
+
+**Reason.** The chain supplies the finished outline. The operator specifies the punch or die side, threading point and physical slug capture. A through cut needs stock at every height. Every unselected primitive, including rejected chain fragments, must clear the full bounding box of the cut, its lead, kerf and released piece. A primitive on a separate layer has the same protection.
+
+**Alternatives dropped.** Reading the entire drawing can cut an unrelated chain. Chords change an analytic arc and move its offset. A stock silhouette cannot verify a sketch's nominal outline. An earlier operation can change the material that holds the piece. This first case uses fresh axis-aligned box stock, one placed drawing and a single vertical pass. The resolver refuses additional bodies and unverified boundary controls.
+
+**Cost to change.** An analytic consumer can use the same selected chain and placement. It must bound its actual leads and kerf, verify its signed offset and prove slug retention. Additional stock shapes, sequential cuts, fixtures and tabs need corresponding material and clearance checks. The project format already stores the stable pick.
+
+The drawing computes its exact primitive signature at construction. An operation status read uses that stored value. Reopening a project reconstructs the signature from its primitives. A coordinate edit below the chain ID quantum changes the signature.
+
+## #3181 - Record the control installed on a machine
+
+**Decision.** Store the installed control model and exact NC software identifier
+in the machine file and in saved projects. The machine can also state its
+installed options and tool-change procedure. An omitted option list means
+unknown. An empty list means the installation has no options. The Machines
+pane shows the stated facts and the post engine exposes them to scripts.
+Saved projects use format version 75. A version 74 project opens with
+these facts unstated. A version 74 reader refuses the newer file so the
+next save cannot silently discard the machine's installation facts.
+
+**Reason.** One controller family spans several releases. Each machine can
+have different options, a tool changer, or a manual change procedure. The post
+owns its supported release list and the syntax of each operation. A newer
+release number alone gives no evidence of compatible output.
+
+**Alternatives.** A controller profile per post in the machine file would tie
+machine records to one output language. A flag per controller feature would
+duplicate the post's syntax rules. Future profiles can share a post by adding
+the documented release and an acceptance test. Existing files can acquire
+installation facts without changing their chosen post.
+
+**First installation.** The Picomax 55 uses the iTNC 530, NC software
+340490-03, in three-axis CNC mode with W fixed before establishing the
+preset. The inventory comes from the installation's 14108131.MP parameters
+and the Fehlmann manuals 141-49-07-E and P07-35-06-E. The machine file omits
+absolute travel bands because their reference frame needs confirmation on
+the machine. It also leaves the installed software options unstated.
+
+**Evidence.** The earlier prototype's one-tool and two-tool programs reached
+the end on the programming station. Its update history identifies release
+`606425 004 SP20`. The converter accepted 41 and 63 blocks respectively.
+These tests apply to those two programs. On the 530, Cycle 247 has no effect during Test Run. The maximum M140 retract can
+end at the active travel limit or stop earlier under collision monitoring.
+Checks on the physical machine still need to establish preset activation,
+the upper change position, the drive-off procedure and coolant operation.
+The [34049x-03 programming manual](https://content.heidenhain.de/doku/tnc_guide/pdf_files/iTNC530/34049x-neu/en/bhb.pdf)
+describes the retract in section 7.4 (page 303) and the Test Run restriction
+in section 8.9 (page 492). The maintainer approved separate machine and post
+changes in [#3098](https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/issues/3098).
+
+## #3182 Heidenhain Klartext controller profiles
+
+Use one Camcraft Klartext post. The machine file records the installed model
+and exact NC software in `controller.identity`, and the tool-change procedure
+in `tooling.tool_change`. Camcraft resolves those facts to a supported language
+profile. The first profile covers the three-axis Fehlmann Picomax 55 with an
+iTNC 530 running `340490-03`. Selecting a newer release needs a documented
+compatibility decision and a test. A release number alone cannot establish
+which options a particular machine has.
+
+Keep the program syntax in the post. Embedding a copy of each cycle and word
+in every machine file would require machine owners to maintain the dialect.
+Using a separate post for every release would duplicate common geometry and
+tool-change checks. A later release can share this profile if its documented
+forms and machine requirements match, or supply a separately tested form
+for each difference.
+
+Limit the first profile to a single metric setup, three linear axes, manual
+tool changes, straight moves, and planar XY arcs. A real stock bottom
+defines the blank even for shallow cuts. Unimplemented operations produce
+a diagnostic. The software checks the emitted program and the refusal paths.
+Commissioning requires separate checks on the target control and machine.
+The manual states that cycle 247 does not activate a preset during Test Run.
+[HEIDENHAIN iTNC 530 conversational programming manual, 533 190-22](https://content.heidenhain.de/doku/tnc_guide/pdf_files/iTNC530/34049x-neu/en/bhb.pdf)
+
+Require the named Picomax installation and an explicit positive spindle range.
+Another mill with the same control software can have different tool-change
+interlocks. Read spindle limits from the machine definition so a restricted
+installation can state a lower maximum. The Picomax file states rapid rates.
+Cutting-feed and machine-coordinate travel limits require independent checks.
+
+Keep each tool number associated with one tool name, type, diameter, and
+corner radius for the whole program. A standalone intermediate file can
+give different tools the same number. Refuse the job before saving it,
+including when a previous tool number returns after a tool change.
+
+The post opts in to a hard refusal for all engine and script warnings, including the standalone CLI. Keeping only script-level refusals could release a program using the previously accepted preset when the engine rejects a later work offset. Other posts retain their existing warning handling. Informational accuracy notes remain in the program header.
+
+## #3184 - Post-merge gate: one independent run per merge
+
+**Decided.** `post-merge.yml` uses one concurrency group for each commit,
+`post-merge-${{ inputs.ref || github.sha }}`, with `cancel-in-progress:
+false`. Each push runs on a free `camcraft-post-merge` runner and always
+finishes. The `gate` job does not change. The `report` job has its own
+group, `post-merge-report` (`cancel-in-progress: false`), and checks out the
+full history (`fetch-depth: 0`). `.github/scripts/post-merge-report.sh`
+keeps the last reported commit in a hidden marker in the rolling issue body,
+`<!-- post-merge-last-sha: <sha> -->`. A run whose commit is a strict
+ancestor of that commit (`git merge-base --is-ancestor`, not the same
+commit) prints a notice and reports nothing.
+
+**Why.** With one group for `main`, a second push waited and a third push
+cancelled the waiting run. A red run then covered several merges, and the
+breaking merge needed a bisect (#3111). With one run for each commit, a red
+run names one commit. Runs can now finish out of order, so the report must
+not let an older green run close the issue of a newer red run.
+
+**Details chosen.**
+- The marker is read from the newest rolling issue, open or closed. A green
+  run with no open issue writes its commit into the closed issue's marker.
+  Without that, an older red run that finishes later would open a new issue
+  after a newer green run.
+- The same commit (a re-run) is not older than itself, and it reports.
+- If git cannot resolve the marker or this commit, the run reports as
+  before (fail open to a report, never to silence).
+- A new red issue carries the marker in its body. A red comment and a green
+  close write the marker into the issue body (the old marker line is
+  replaced).
+
+**Dropped.**
+- A repository variable or a separate state file for the last sha: the
+  issue asks for no new secrets or variables, and the rolling issue is
+  already the one shared state.
+- Comparing commit dates instead of ancestry: dates of merge commits are not
+  an order guarantee.
+
+**Known limit.** GitHub keeps at most one pending job in a concurrency
+group. If three report jobs wait at once, GitHub cancels the middle one.
+That report is lost; the next run on `main` reports the newer state. With
+two runners and a gate of about 20 min, three reports at once are rare.
+
+**Cost to change later.** Low. The marker is one line in the issue body;
+removing the guard returns the old behaviour.
+
+## #3206 - wire-iso writes each dimension word with a decimal point
+
+**Decided:** the generic ISO wire post (`assets/posts/wire-iso.cps`)
+always writes the decimal point on X, Y, U, V, I, J and the taper angle
+word: `X5.`, `Y0.`, `U0.`, `A3.`. Trailing zeros are still removed
+(`X-20.165`, `X0.5`), and a negative zero writes `0.`. Comment text
+(the taper note `Z12`) keeps its plain numbers.
+
+**Why:** many wire controls read a number without a point in their
+least input unit (0.001 mm). On such a control `X5` moves 5 um, not
+5 mm. A word with a point reads the same on a control of either
+convention, so the point is always safe. The Sodick post already uses
+the same rule.
+
+**Dropped:** a machine or post option that switches the point on or off.
+No control we know needs the point removed, and an option adds a way to
+get wrong motion. If a control that refuses `X5.` appears, add the option
+then, with the point as its default.
+
+**Cost to change later:** low. The rule is one helper (`pt`) in the post
+script; the eight wire-iso goldens move by the point only.
+
+## #3208 - Keep strict wire cover checks without arc provenance
+
+**Decided.** The wire cover law (`crates/motion/src/wire_edm/laws.rs`)
+restores the upstream strict `COVER_TOL` of 0.002 mm for all current
+polygon profiles. Remove
+the allowance that inferred meshed rounds from vertex coordinates.
+An allowance requires reliable arc or mesh provenance. The current
+`Loop` and wire generator inputs contain points, without that provenance.
+
+**Why.** The review of PR #3224 found a sharp 16-sided R0.5 polygon
+that passes every geometric guard of the inferred-round classifier.
+Each corner turns 22.5 degrees, and its chord sag is about 0.0096 mm.
+With a 0.162 mm kerf offset, the path leaves about 0.0032 mm at each
+corner. This exceeds the 0.002 mm cover tolerance. The same points can
+represent an authored polygon or a tessellated arc. Their coordinates
+cannot establish which profile the user intended.
+
+The original coarse-fillet acceptance remains deferred. A coarse mesh
+without provenance must receive the same strict check as an authored
+polygon. Fine facets that meet the existing cover tolerance can pass.
+
+**Dropped.**
+
+- Infer arc provenance from turn, radius, sag, or matching neighbours.
+  The small drawn polygon satisfies these tests and remains unsafe.
+- Increase `COVER_TOL`. This also accepts sharp corners with excessive
+  material left standing.
+- Add a caller-controlled allowance flag. A flag alone does not prove
+  that the input came from a true arc or a model mesh.
+- Add an end-to-end provenance API in this repair. The requested repair
+  is limited to the review findings. Reliable provenance needs separate
+  design and validation through the profile extraction path.
+
+**Not changed.** The path remains the offset of the polygon chords.
+The generator refuses a path that exceeds the existing cover tolerance.
+No fixture, golden file, importer, or post processor changes are needed.
+
+**Cost to change later.** A future allowance must carry reliable source
+information through profile extraction to the cover check. Its tests
+must distinguish authored polygons from supported source arcs or meshes.
+
+## #3209 - Wire 4-axis: stitched runs and a die face's opening are profiles
+
+**What was decided.**
+
+- The 4-axis wire cut groups the picked open runs by plane. The runs of
+  one plane go through the stitcher that the 2-axis cuts use
+  (`stitch_open_runs`). When they close into exactly one ring and every
+  run is in that ring, the ring is the profile of that plane.
+- Runs that do not close are refused by name ("do not close into one
+  ring"). Runs that close into two or more rings are refused by name.
+- One open run alone on a plane stays open. The generator refuses it as
+  before (an open profile has no die or punch side).
+- A face with more than one closed ring gives its one opening (a `Hole`
+  ring) when the operation is on the die side. In all other cases a
+  face with more than one ring is refused, and the text now says that
+  the edge chains of one ring are stitched.
+- The stitcher runs once for each plane, never over all picks. Two
+  outlines of the same shape at two heights have the same XY ends, and a
+  walk over all picks could weld a top run to a bottom run.
+
+**Why.** The issue's three refusals sent the user in a circle: the face
+refusal said "pick the edge chain", and the chain refusal said "pick one
+face". The 2-axis cut already stitches the same picks, so the 4-axis cut
+uses the same weld law and the same tolerance.
+
+**Dropped.**
+
+- A punch face with an opening reads its outer ring. Dropped: the punch
+  side states no rule for which ring of two it cuts, and a guess can cut
+  the wrong outline. The user picks the edge chains of the ring.
+- A face with several openings on the die side picks the largest one.
+  Dropped for the same reason.
+- Curved wall patches as profiles. Dropped: a wall is not a plane
+  outline. The refusal now names what to pick instead.
+
+**Cost to change later.** Small. The rules are in `read`, `plane_ring`
+and `profile` of `crates/app/src/operation/wire_ruled.rs`.
+
+## #3211 - The wire gouge depth reads how far the wire stands inside the part
+
+**Decided.** The kerf law reads a signed clearance (`dist::entry`). When a
+move stands in the product, the reading is minus the distance from the
+move's deepest point to the product's boundary. The gouge depth is then the
+kerf reach plus that distance. Before, the clearance was 0 for such a move,
+so each gouge read the kerf offset alone (0.162 mm on a 2.8 mm entry).
+
+**Why.** The verify message names a depth and "the deepest reading at any
+height". An operator uses that number to decide what to change (the verify
+tolerance, the model, the path). A depth that is always the kerf offset
+hides how far the cut enters the part. The verdict stays the same: each
+move that gouged before still gouges, and the post stays blocked.
+
+**How the deepest point is found.** Split the move at each product boundary
+crossing, including the stock boundary for a die. Search every inside
+interval with endpoint bounds on its 1-Lipschitz boundary distance. Refine
+an interval while its bound can exceed the best reading by more than the
+move length divided by 1024. The reported depth underestimates the deepest
+entry by at most that amount, apart from floating-point roundoff. The
+reported point is inside the product. An excluded stock boundary can hold
+the supremum; the search approaches it from inside to the same tolerance.
+The two move ends are read exactly when they belong to the product.
+
+The former coarse walk and local refinement could miss a whole inside
+interval. Its stated error bound was false. The review's 100 mm move
+through the square at X=[50.5625, 52.5625] now reads a 1 mm entry. Global
+interval bounds replace that local search; another deeper interval cannot
+be discarded because its coarse samples are outside.
+
+**Dropped.**
+- An exact maximum through the medial axis of the section: much more code
+  for a number that only sizes a refused gouge.
+- A change to the threading and open-end laws: they need only "in or not
+  clear", and they say "at the point itself" for 0. They stay on the
+  unsigned `clearance`.
+- A change to which move the finding names: it stays the first move that
+  gouges at the first plane, with the deepest reading over all planes.
+
+**Cost to change later.** One function in `crates/sim/src/verify/wire/dist.rs`
+and its two callers.
+
+## #3212 - Use one MCP key for EDM codes
+
+**Chosen:** MCP machine definitions use `edm.codes` in both `edit_machine`
+and `get_job`. The internal `Machine.edm_codes` storage stays unchanged.
+The MCP edit rejects the legacy top-level `edm_codes` key by name.
+The edit reuses `codes_sane` to validate code names and text before mounting.
+
+**Why:** Machine files and wire post diagnostics already name `edm.codes`.
+One visible key lets callers edit the same definition that they read.
+Rejecting the storage key prevents two competing maps in one patch.
+The existing undoable mount remains the command that applies the edit.
+
+**Dropped:** A change to the shared serde schema would also change embedded
+project machine definitions. This fix needs only an MCP view conversion.
+An alias for the top-level key would add precedence rules without a need.
+
+**Cost to change later:** A shared schema change needs project compatibility
+tests. An alias needs an explicit conflict rule and MCP tests.
+
+## #3213 - Remove unsupported characters from Sodick comments
+
+**Decided:** Replace `#`, `[`, `]` and backslash with spaces in the Sodick
+post's `note()` function.
+
+**Why:** Every comment route, including program notes, passes through this
+function. This keeps the existing comment lengths and diagnostic text.
+The existing `clean()` function handles names and limits them to 48
+characters. Applying that limit to all comments would shorten static warnings.
+
+**Dropped:** Changing the shared sanitizer would affect other dialects.
+Rejecting names or shortening all comments would add restrictions that this
+fix does not need.
+
+**Cost to change later:** Change the Sodick post and its comment golden.
